From: Fix VFIO migration cmask validation failure
Subject: [BUGFIX] Add pre_load callback to limit cmask for normal migration
Date: 2026-05-15

Bug Description:
VFIO normal migration fails with PCI config space validation error when
hardware read-only bits differ between source and target devices.

Error example:
  get_pci_config_device: Bad config data: i=0x4a read: 0 device: 8 cmask: ff wmask: 0

Root Cause:
VFIO migration checks config space bits using cmask. Hardware read-only
bits (wmask=0) should not be checked because:
1. Source and target are different physical devices
2. Hardware bits naturally differ (device state, capabilities)
3. Checking them causes false validation failures

CPR migration handles this correctly via vfio_cpr_pci_pre_load() which
limits cmask to emulated_config_bits only. Normal migration lacks this.

Fix:
Add pre_load callback to vmstate_vfio_pci_config that limits cmask to
only check emulated bits, ignoring hardware read-only bits.

Note:
This mirrors the CPR fix in cpr.c:99-111. The comment there explains:
"The kernel may change non-emulated config bits. Exclude them from
the changed-bits check."

---

 hw/vfio/pci.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/vfio/pci.c b/hw/vfio/pci.c
index xxx..xxx 100644
--- a/hw/vfio/pci.c
+++ b/hw/vfio/pci.c
@@ -2860,6 +2860,27 @@ static const VMStateDescription vmstate_vfio_display = {
 };
 #endif
 
+/*
+ * vfio_pci_pre_load - Prepare for config space loading
+ *
+ * The kernel/hardware may change non-emulated config bits between
+ * source and target devices. These are hardware read-only bits that
+ * reflect device state and capabilities, which naturally differ.
+ *
+ * We limit cmask to only check emulated_config_bits, excluding
+ * hardware bits from validation to prevent false failures.
+ *
+ * This mirrors vfio_cpr_pci_pre_load() for normal migration.
+ */
+static int vfio_pci_pre_load(void *opaque)
+{
+    VFIOPCIDevice *vdev = opaque;
+    PCIDevice *pdev = PCI_DEVICE(vdev);
+    int size = MIN(pci_config_size(pdev), vdev->config_size);
+    int i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < size; i++) {
+        pdev->cmask[i] &= vdev->emulated_config_bits[i];
+    }
+
+    return 0;
+}
+
 static const VMStateDescription vmstate_vfio_pci_config = {
     .name = "VFIOPCIDevice",
     .version_id = 1,
     .minimum_version_id = 1,
+    .pre_load = vfio_pci_pre_load,
     .fields = (const VMStateField[]) {
         VMSTATE_PCI_DEVICE(parent_obj, VFIOPCIDevice),
         VMSTATE_MSIX_TEST(parent_obj, VFIOPCIDevice, vfio_msix_present),
         VMSTATE_END_OF_LIST()
     },
     .subsections = (const VMStateDescription * const []) {
         &vmstate_vfio_display,
         NULL
     }
 };
 
 static int vfio_pci_save_config(VFIODevice *vbasedev, QEMUFile *f, Error **errp)

---

Technical Details:

cmask (change mask): Which bits to check for changes during migration
wmask (write mask): Which bits are writable by guest (0 = hardware bits)
emulated_config_bits: Which bits are emulated by QEMU (vs read from hardware)

Validation logic (pci.c:815-816):
    if ((config[i] ^ s->config[i]) &
        s->cmask[i] & ~s->wmask[i] & ~s->w1cmask[i])

This checks: cmask bits that are hardware read-only.

Problem: If cmask=ff and wmask=0, ALL bits are checked.
Solution: Limit cmask to emulated bits only.

Example scenario:
- Source device: config[0x4a] = 0 (saved in migration file)
- Target device: config[0x4a] = 8 (hardware state at init)
- cmask[0x4a] = ff (check all bits) - BEFORE fix
- cmask[0x4a] = emulated_bits (check emulated only) - AFTER fix
- If emulated_bits[0x4a] = 0 (hardware bits only), validation passes

This fix allows hardware read-only bits to differ naturally without
causing migration failures, while still validating QEMU-emulated bits.

